ns1:domestic_proceeding_17 a ns1:DomesticProceeding ;
    rdfs:label "police procedure in which applicant was confronted, searched, and escaped (paragraph 17)"^^xsd:string ;
    ns1:hasSourceParagraph 17 ;
    ns1:hasSupportingQuote "When the driver, who was later identified as the applicant, stopped the car, an unspecified number of the Special Unit’s officers confronted him and informed him that this was a police procedure. The applicant jumped towards one of the officers and attempted to hit him. The officer managed to block his blow and then with the other officers forced the applicant to lean against his car in order to search him. The applicant struggled free and escaped."^^xsd:string .

ns1:domestic_proceeding_18 a ns1:DomesticProceeding ;
    rdfs:label "police procedure where applicant was detained, searched, force used, and released after questioning (paragraph 18)"^^xsd:string ;
    ns1:followsProceeding ns1:domestic_proceeding_17 ;
    ns1:hasSourceParagraph 18 ;
    ns1:hasSupportingQuote "When the officers caught him, the applicant again tried to resist and they responded by using truncheons and gripped his elbow to handcuff him, knocking him down in the process. Since the applicant continued to resist, the officers tied his wrists with a plastic cord. They subsequently took him to the police station on suspicion of committing the criminal offence of “obstructing an official in the course of his duties” ( preprečitev uradnega dejanja uradni osebi ). The applicant was released after questioning."^^xsd:string .

ns1:domestic_proceeding_19 a ns1:DomesticProceeding ;
    rdfs:label "authorization and later submissions regarding use of force by officers during police procedure (paragraph 19)"^^xsd:string ;
    ns1:followsProceeding ns1:domestic_proceeding_18 ;
    ns1:hasSourceParagraph 19 ;
    ns1:hasSupportingQuote "The officers were authorised to use force on the basis of section 54 of the Internal Affairs Act. During the operation, they wore vests with the visible sign “Police” (“ Policija ”). In their later submissions, they stated that only two officers had used force against the applicant."^^xsd:string .

ns1:domestic_proceeding_20 a ns1:DomesticProceeding ;
    rdfs:label "police procedure conduct regarding medical aid and applicant’s complaint (paragraph 20)"^^xsd:string ;
    ns1:followsProceeding ns1:domestic_proceeding_19 ;
    ns1:hasSourceParagraph 20 ;
    ns1:hasSupportingQuote "During the police procedure, the applicant did not request medical aid and the police only afterwards learned that he had sustained injuries. In addition, he did not complain about the conduct of the officers during the procedure."^^xsd:string .

ns1:domestic_proceeding_21 a ns1:DomesticProceeding ;
    rdfs:label "criminal proceedings judgment establishing police information and order to stop applicant’s car (paragraph 21)"^^xsd:string ;
    ns1:followsProceeding ns1:domestic_proceeding_20 ;
    ns1:hasSourceParagraph 21 ;
    ns1:hasSupportingQuote "The judgment of 12 February 2001 issued in the proceedings instituted against the applicant (see paragraph 48), established that three officers of the Slovenj Gradec Police, D.P., J.K. and M.F., who were in the car which stopped the applicant, received information about the location of a “white Golf”, believed to be being driven by the applicant, and an order to stop it."^^xsd:string .

ns1:domestic_proceeding_22 a ns1:DomesticProceeding ;
    rdfs:label "criminal investigation testimony of Officer J.K. about information and vehicles present (paragraph 22)"^^xsd:string ;
    ns1:followsProceeding ns1:domestic_proceeding_21 ;
    ns1:hasSourceParagraph 22 ;
    ns1:hasSupportingQuote "Officer J.K. testified in the criminal investigation instituted against the applicant, that they had received information that the “white Golf” had left “the place”, which meant that it had left Pik Bar . He was not able to comment on the speed of the applicant’s car. In addition, he stated that by the time a minute had gone by after the applicant was stopped, there were already four or five vehicles of the Slovenj Gradec Police and the Special Unit at the scene and that three or four officers arrived in each vehicle."^^xsd:string .

ns1:domestic_proceeding_23 a ns1:DomesticProceeding ;
    rdfs:label "criminal investigation testimony of Officer M.F. regarding suspicions and attempted stop despite warnings (paragraph 23)"^^xsd:string ;
    ns1:followsProceeding ns1:domestic_proceeding_22 ;
    ns1:hasSourceParagraph 23 ;
    ns1:hasSupportingQuote """Officer M.F. stated in the same criminal investigation:
“We had information about individuals who were suspected members of that [criminal] organisation, and one of them was Aleksander Matko, whom I did not know since I am from Maribor, but I knew him from photographs. (...) We were on one of the streets of Slovenj Gradec when we received information that the car of Aleksander Matko had been seen and that our colleagues had tried to stop him, but he would not stop despite warnings.”"""^^xsd:string .

ns1:domestic_proceeding_24 a ns1:DomesticProceeding ;
    rdfs:label "criminal investigation testimony of Officer D.P. about organised crime operation and decision to stop/search (paragraph 24)"^^xsd:string ;
    ns1:followsProceeding ns1:domestic_proceeding_23 ;
    ns1:hasSourceParagraph 24 ;
    ns1:hasSupportingQuote """Officer D.P. explained in his testimony in the above mentioned criminal investigation:
“At the critical time, we were conducting, in the territory of Slovenj Gradec Police, an operation to investigate organised crime. (...) In front of the Hotel Pohorje, a group of people was noticed which included M.A. and Aleksander Matko. One of the police patrols noticed that M.A. left with the motorcycle and they also saw when Aleksander Matko drove away. In fact, everything happened very quickly (...). We placed vehicles at different locations (...). Our official vehicle, which I was driving, received a message that a white Golf was being driven by a person believed to be Aleksander Matko (...). We decided to stop him in order to search the car since there was a suspicion that he was armed.”"""^^xsd:string .

ns1:domestic_proceeding_25 a ns1:DomesticProceeding ;
    rdfs:label "criminal investigation statement of I.G. about number of officers and direct contact (paragraph 25)"^^xsd:string ;
    ns1:followsProceeding ns1:domestic_proceeding_24 ;
    ns1:hasSourceParagraph 25 ;
    ns1:hasSupportingQuote "Another officer from the Slovenj Gradec Police, I.G., who had arrived at the scene with the officers of the Special Unit and was also questioned in the criminal investigation, stated that there were 20 officers of the Special Unit and Slovenj Gradec Police at the scene, and that five of them had had direct contact with the applicant."^^xsd:string .

ns1:domestic_proceeding_26 a ns1:DomesticProceeding ;
    rdfs:label "medical admission after release at Slovenj Gradec General Hospital (paragraph 26)"^^xsd:string ;
    ns1:followsProceeding ns1:domestic_proceeding_25 ;
    ns1:hasSourceParagraph 26 ;
    ns1:hasSupportingQuote "On 6 April 1995, soon after his release, the applicant was admitted to the Slovenj Gradec General Hospital where he stayed until 7 p.m. The medical report, written by a doctor in that hospital, stated that the applicant had bruises on his head, but did not include any details of the injuries. The applicant was advised to rest for a few days."^^xsd:string .

